# Fictional Band Name: Copper Mile Fellowship

## Band Description

Copper Mile Fellowship is a five-member Ugandan-American soul ensemble built around the endingidi as an emotional response voice inside a disciplined Southern pocket.

**Nansubuga Achieng** — endingidi, Wire Reply phrasing, Backbeat Lean, One-String Turnarounds.

**Calvin Brooks** — Wurlitzer, arranging, vocal direction, harmonic architecture.

**Joel Ssemakula** — electric bass, pocket design, melodic pickups, low-register restraint.

**Ruth Nakato** — clean tremolo guitar, muted chord fragments, counter-riffs, backing vocals.

**Marcus Reed** — drum kit, engalabi, handclap architecture, dynamic control.

Baritone saxophone is performed by a rotating sixth chair on recordings and larger live shows, preserving the band’s core five-person identity while allowing Hornless Lift sections to expand physically when needed.

There is no conventional virtuoso hierarchy. Nansubuga carries the emotional after-sentence; Calvin shapes the harmonic room around it; Joel and Marcus make the pocket deep enough that late fiddle arrivals never sound rhythmically lost; Ruth protects the middle register by ending her phrases before the bow speaks. Live versions vary because bow pressure, singer timing, room humidity, bass-note length, handclap placement, and the exact width of each endingidi slide change the emotional weight of the same chart.

