# Fictional EP Tracklist

## Track 1: The Bus Leaves Before the Bow Arrives

**Story & Context:** At 88 BPM, Joel opens with a two-bar bass figure using tonic, fifth, sixth, and a chromatic pickup into the IV chord while Marcus holds a dry kick-snare pocket slightly behind the grid. Calvin plays compact Wurlitzer dominant-9 and major-6 voicings. The singer delivers a short verse about missing the final district bus by less than a minute. Nansubuga remains silent until the final two words of each line, then enters with Wire Replies that begin below the sung pitch and arrive late into the backbeat. The hook uses Backbeat Lean deliberately: the bow attack begins before beat four but the target major third is not reached until just after the snare.

The bridge introduces Hornless Lift. Baritone saxophone hits one low tonic on the first beat of every second bar while the endingidi bends above it through a four-note contour. No trumpet, tenor saxophone, or organ swell appears. The section becomes larger by widening register rather than adding layers.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Kampala roadside bus stage at sunset, one bus already disappearing around a bend while a person stands under a faded route sign holding a folded paper ticket, warm dust in the air, copper and green palette, documentary realism. Sparse lyric fragments: “bus gone early,” “one minute late,” “I heard the engine turn,” “naye nkiri wano / but I am still here.”

## Track 2: Receipt Folded Into Four Corners

**Story & Context:** At 96 BPM, the EP’s tightest groove begins with Ruth’s muted tremolo guitar and close handclaps before the full kit enters. Calvin uses a minor-7 to dominant-9 movement with wide rests between voicings. The lyric centers on a market receipt kept for years because a name was written on the back. Nansubuga’s endingidi part is built almost entirely from One-String Turnarounds: she waits through each sung phrase and takes only the final two beats of every four-bar unit.

Joel becomes busier in the second verse, so the endingidi simplifies to one bowed fall or a two-note response. Marcus adds engalabi only in the pre-chorus, placing one open center stroke before the handclap pair. The final chorus shifts into Bow-Breath Exchange: baritone saxophone sustains low fifths while Nansubuga switches to short bowed pickups, then the roles reverse for the last eight bars.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Close-up of a worn produce-market receipt folded into four precise corners, one handwritten name visible on the reverse through thin paper, resting beside coins and a green weighing-scale tray under tungsten light. Sparse lyric fragments: “four corners,” “your name behind the price,” “I never threw it out,” “bika bulungi / keep it safe.”

## Track 3: Copper Road After Closing Time

**Story & Context:** The closer drops to 80 BPM. Marcus uses kick, rim, and occasional engalabi body strokes rather than a full snare backbeat for the first minute. Calvin holds slow Wurlitzer major-6 and suspended voicings. Joel plays no more than six bass notes per four bars. Ruth lets tremolo guitar chords decay fully.

Nansubuga enters with the longest endingidi phrases on the EP, but she never turns them into a solo. Each phrase still answers something: a vocal word, a Wurlitzer chord, a guitar harmonic, or a baritone-saxophone breath. In the middle section, the singer stops entirely and Calvin plays one two-note figure every four bars. The endingidi answers with increasingly narrow slides until the distance between approach pitch and destination becomes almost microscopic.

The final minute removes baritone saxophone, handclaps, and Wurlitzer one by one. Joel holds a low tonic while Marcus returns to a simple snare on beats two and four. Nansubuga performs one final Backbeat Lean, beginning below the pitch before beat four and reaching the tonic only after the snare has already faded. The EP ends on bow release and room tone rather than a chord cutoff.

